#     path; unchanged by this patch (present verbatim in ovsrc-m17b and in the
#     pristine upstream tree at the pin) and not reached by anything this patch
#     touches. INVESTIGATED 2026-09-05, harmless, no change made: that memory
#     object is the down matmul's binary post-op input (onednn_matmul::type::
#     with_bin_mul_per_row -> post_op_bin_mul(false), grouped_matmul_helper.hpp),
#     whose descriptor is baked into the primitive at creation as {m_M, 1} with
#     m_M = n_token -- exactly the n_token contiguous elements the gather kernel
#     wrote. onednn_linear::forward() binds the caller's memory object verbatim
#     as DNNL_ARG_ATTR_MULTIPLE_POST_OP(0)|DNNL_ARG_SRC_1, and oneDNN's execute
#     path (cvt_primitive_args, thirdparty/onednn_gpu/src/common/
#     primitive_exec_types.cpp) only classifies and counts arguments through
#     pd->arg_usage(); it never compares a bound memory's descriptor with the
#     primitive's, so the over-declared 1-D extent is never read from, nor even
#     inspected. It cannot overrun the buffer either: scratch.routing_weights is
#     allocated at max_batch = max_topk * token_num (get_internal_buffer_descs),
#     and n_token <= token_num because a token routes to a given expert at most
#     once, so n_token * max_topk <= max_batch always. At the shapes served
#     (num_experts_per_tok 10 for the coder, 8 for the 35B) the declaration is
#     10x/8x the consumed n_token, all of it inside the allocation -- consistent
#     with every equivalence gate on this path passing byte-identical. The only
#     conceivable exposure is a future oneDNN that starts validating post-op
